course: dictionary
lesson: 926
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The word bless is a good example. Read the words around it to find out which meaning the writer means.
explain: «On the seventh day God rested and blessed the day.» Here blessed means asked God to make the day holy and good. The words God and day are the clues.
explain: «Bless you!» after a sneeze is not about God at all. It is a polite thing to say, and people often reply «thank you». The clue is the sneeze.
explain: To be blessed with something means you are lucky to have it, as in «blessed with good health». Put your meaning in the sentence to check: lucky to have good health makes sense, so you have found the right one.
fact: Bless is a verb, so its forms change: bless, blesses, blessed or blest, and blessing.
fact: «Bless» can mean to ask God to protect someone or bring them good, often said by a priest or religious leader.
fact: «Bless you!» is what people say when someone sneezes, and the reply is often «thank you».
fact: To be «blessed with» something means to be lucky to have it.
fact: In UK English, «bless him» or «bless her» shows love or sympathy for someone small or sweet.
exercise: «On the seventh day God rested and blessed the day.» What does blessed mean here? | Put away for later | Lucky to have it | *Asked God to make it holy and good | Sneezed loudly
exercise: Someone sneezes and you say «Bless you!». What is this a polite way of doing? | Telling them to sit down | *Showing good manners after a sneeze | Asking God for money | Saying goodbye
exercise: «She is blessed with good health.» What does this sentence mean? | She is sad about her health | She has to pay for her health | *She is lucky to have good health | She asks God for health every day
test: Which meaning of bless is used in «blessed with good health»? | To cry | To ask God to protect | *To be lucky to have something | To sneeze
test: In UK English, someone says «Bless him, he is so small!» What does it show? | *Love or sympathy | Boredom | Surprise | Anger
exam: «The priest will bless the new church.» What does bless mean here? | *Ask God to protect and bring good to | Sneeze at | Count the people in | Get rid of
